          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Abraham:</strong> Two of the main areas that can be vulnerable to attack from cyber criminals are the hardware and software of a computer system so let’s have a look at these two areas and how they can be affected.</p>
<p>Hardware is the physical components of a computer system and covers everything a system needs to function including computer chips, motherboard, graphics card, CPU [central processing unit], ventilation fan, RAM, HDD, webcam and power supply etc. Larger organisations have computer systems involving large servers such as mainframes integrated with storage.</p>
<p>All electronic devices including smart appliances, computers and mobile devices, to large computer systems are run by semiconductor integrated circuits, also known as computer chips and these can be at risk during the design and manufacturing process, of being altered maliciously. Malicious hardware in the form of chips can remain dormant for months or years before being used to launch an attack. Often security in 'chip' production is not always looked upon as an issue, but this form of hardware vulnerability can affect devices from smart phones to major defence systems.</p>
<p>An example of this was the Spectre and Meltdown vulnerabilities that were identified in 2018. These were design flaws in chips that have been in production since 1995 that meant criminals could bypass some of the most fundamental low-level security mechanisms and access things like passwords and encryption keys.</p>
<p>The Operating system or OS is the software that enables computer hardware to communicate and operate all the other software that is subsequently loaded onto the computer. Every computer must have at least one operating system to run other programs. It speaks the computers language and therefore talks to the software.</p>
<p>The operating system has many functions including managing memory, files, processes, system and devices and Security of course. Operating systems should always be kept up to date to maintain that security, so regular updates should be scheduled to protect from malware threat and unauthorised access.</p>
<p>As well as keeping the OS updated other software should be kept up to date so that any bugs or patches can be added to keep the software malware free. There are a range of different operating systems available, and these will depend on the type of computer system being used.</p>
<p>All computers should have a security system in place with antimalware software. This may be part of an internet supplier package including firewalls and VPN. Or it may be a standalone antimalware software package which should be regularly updated or run continuously. This will seek out any threats that may affect a computer system. Some users stick to free antimalware software, but this does not protect a system as much as paid for programs. Antimalware packages should protect systems from the latest virus, malware, spyware, and ransomware attacks whilst maintaining a user’s privacy and identity especially when browsing the web.</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
  
